  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis shows an area of low pressure moving\neast over northern Lower Michigan with a cold front extending \nsouthwest into the central Plains. Surface high pressure centered \nover the Dakotas is building eastward, driving a cold advection \nregime with brisk northwest winds and widespread cloud cover \nacross northern Wisconsin. Upstream, a deep upper-level low is \npositioned over Nevada while shortwave energy ejects across the \nCentral Rockies toward the Upper Midwest. Cold advection behind \nthe front will keep conditions mostly cloudy and seasonably cool \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nLight Rain And Lingering Stratus Through Wednesday: As weak \nshortwave energy ejects from the western CONUS trough this \nevening, a 20 percent chance of light rain showers will traverse \ncentral and northeast Wisconsin. A pronounced mid- level dry wedge\nbetween 5,000 and 8,000 feet will rainfall intensity, keeping NBM\n90th percentile QPF under 0.05 inches area- wide. Low stratus \nwill surge southward overnight into Wednesday morning, getting \ntrapped beneath a persistent subsidence inversion. High pressure \nbuilding into the region Wednesday will maintain mostly cloudy \nskies with daytime high temperatures ranging from the middle 50s \nto middle 60s.\n\nWidespread Rain Thursday Into Thursday Night: Attention then \nshifts to an amplifying northern stream shortwave and associated \nsurface low tracking northeast across the Great Lakes Thursday \ninto Thursday night. Strong dynamic forcing and moisture transport\nwith precipitable water values reaching 180 percent of normal \nwill spread widespread light to moderate rain across the region \nafter noon Thursday. NBM probabilistic guidance reflects high \nconfidence in widespread measurable rain, showing a 40 to 60 \npercent probability of 24-hour QPF exceeding 0.50 inches. Rain \nwill transition to scattered showers Thursday night before the \nprimary low exits eastward early Friday morning.\n\nStrong Friday Winds And Showers: Cold advection surges across the\nwestern Great Lakes Friday morning in the wake of the departing \ncyclone, dropping 850mb temperatures over western Lake Superior to\nnear -5 degrees C. Steep low-level lapse rates and a tight \nsurface pressure gradient will yield strong, gusty northwest \nwinds, with NBM guidance indicating a 40 to 70 percent probability\nof gusts exceeding 40 mph. Over the Green Bay nearshore zones, \nconfidence is increasing for gale-force wind gusts. Strong thermal\ninstability over the lake waters will maintain scattered rain \nshowers through Friday afternoon before surface high pressure \nbuilds in from the west.\n\nWidespread Friday Night Freeze And Frost Risk: Canadian surface\nhigh pressure settling directly over Wisconsin Friday night will\nestablish a very chilly airmass across the region beneath \nclearing skies and decoupling winds. Overnight minimum \ntemperatures are forecast to plummet into the middle 20s to lower \n30s across inland north- central and central Wisconsin, with \nmid-30s near the Lake Michigan shore. NBM probabilistic guidance \ndisplays high confidence in a widespread freeze, showing a 70 to \n90 percent chance of minimum temperatures dropping below 32 \ndegrees west of the Fox Valley. Rapid diurnal warming beneath \nfull sunshine Saturday morning will quickly burn off widespread \nfrost, with temperatures rebounding into the upper 50s and 60s for\nthe weekend.",
